WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.640
Fox's hit musical dramedy "Glee" made fans laugh,&nbsp;
cry, and sing along for six award-winning seasons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:10.520
But not all of the performers who brought&nbsp;
the show to life are still with us today.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:15.680
Cory Monteith played Finn Hudson, an all-American&nbsp;
jock with a sensitive side and soothing voice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:19.800
Finn's on-again, off-again relationship with&nbsp;
Lea Michele's Rachel Berry was a centerpiece&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:23.736
of the show's dramatic moments, earning&nbsp;
the affectionate portmanteau "Finchel."

00:00:23.736 --> 00:00:29.080
However, Monteith came to the Glee cast with some&nbsp;
weighty personal struggles. In a 2011 interview&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:32.800
with Parade, the Canadian actor opened up&nbsp;
about his battle with drugs and alcohol,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:37.440
which began when he was just 13 years old.&nbsp;
By the time he was 19, Monteith's friends and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.880
family organized an intervention, after which&nbsp;
the actor entered rehab for the first time.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:46.240
Unfortunately, Monteith's demons continued&nbsp;
to haunt him throughout his 20s. According&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:51.720
to E! Online, he entered rehab again in 2013,&nbsp;
completing the program at the end of April. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:56.360
on July 14, news broke that Monteith was&nbsp;
found dead in his Vancouver hotel room.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:01:00.360
Autopsy results revealed that the actor&nbsp;
died of an accidental overdose of heroin and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:04.880
alcohol with traces of codeine and morphine&nbsp;
also found in his blood. The third episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:08.800
of the fifth season of Glee, titled "The&nbsp;
Quarterback," was dedicated to Monteith.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:10.960
"Everyone wants to talk about how he died too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:15.880
but who cares? One moment in his whole&nbsp;
life, I care more about how he lived."

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:20.080
Every show about high school needs a "bad boy."&nbsp;
For Glee, that was Mark Salling's character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:24.120
Noah "Puck" Puckerman. Salling appeared in&nbsp;
the show for the entirety of the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:27.360
though he was only a recurring&nbsp;
character for its final two seasons.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:31.040
Much like Shakespeare's character of the&nbsp;
same name, Puck was a mischief-maker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:34.400
albeit a lovable one. As the&nbsp;
series' resident deviant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:38.240
Puck was known for his sexual exploits&nbsp;
and violent tendencies. Like his co-star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:41.720
Cory Monteith, Salling also found&nbsp;
himself in unflattering headlines.

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:46.120
In 2013, Salling's ex-girlfriend,&nbsp;
Roxanne Gorzela, sued the Texas native,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:50.800
alleging he forced her to have unprotected&nbsp;
sex. Though Salling denied the accusations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:55.360
TMZ reports he paid Gorzela a&nbsp;
settlement of $2.7 million out of court.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.720
A few months after Glee ended,&nbsp;
Salling was arrested and charged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:02.680
with possession of child pornography. When&nbsp;
authorities searched his California home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:07.240
they discovered more than 50,000 images of&nbsp;
child pornography on his computer. Salling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:12.280
pleaded guilty in December 2017, with&nbsp;
sentencing set for March 2018. Likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:18.880
facing several years in prison, Salling died by&nbsp;
suicide in January 2018. He was 35 years old.

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:23.160
Jane Lynch is one of only five Glee actors to&nbsp;
star in a main role for all six seasons of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:27.480
show. As Sue Sylvester, head coach of William&nbsp;
McKinley High School's cheerleading squad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:30.600
Lynch played the show's villain.&nbsp;
Typically clad in a tracksuit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:34.840
Sylvester stalked the halls of McKinley,&nbsp;
instilling fear in both students and staff.

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:38.400
Despite her edges, however, Sue also&nbsp;
had a soft spot for Becky Jackson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:40.920
a McKinley student with Down's&nbsp;
syndrome who she took under her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.720
wing. Though her affection for&nbsp;
Becky seems out of character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:48.080
viewers eventually learn that Sue has an older&nbsp;
sister, Jean, who also has Down's syndrome.

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:51.080
"There's really only one person&nbsp;
in the world I wanna impress."

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:51.720
"Who?"

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:53.800
"Who? You silly!"

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:57.640
Robin Trocki, who came to Glee with no&nbsp;
acting experience, played Jean Sylvester&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:02.680
in five episodes throughout Seasons 1 and 2.&nbsp;
Trocki died in 2019 from Alzheimer's disease&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:06.680
at the age of 63. While not a star of&nbsp;
the series, Trocki's involvement with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:11.120
Glee helped raise awareness for Down's&nbsp;
syndrome in a nearly unprecedented way.

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:15.360
For the first season of Glee, Naya Rivera&nbsp;
played the recurring character Santana Lopez,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:19.360
one of Sue's top cheerleaders, before being&nbsp;
promoted to the show's main cast in Season&nbsp;2. 

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:23.200
Just as Mark Salling's character filled&nbsp;
the role of "bad boy," Rivera's character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:26.480
served as the "mean girl," though&nbsp;
that persona softened over time.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:30.480
In addition to providing powerful vocals&nbsp;
in New Directions, Santana also added to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:35.440
the show's LGBT sensibilities. Early in the&nbsp;
series, Santana is revealed to be bisexual,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:38.880
and in Season 6 she marries fellow&nbsp;
glee club alum Brittany Pierce.

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:44.120
In July 2020, Rivera took her 4-year-old son&nbsp;
on a pontoon boat in California's Lake Piru.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:48.040
When Rivera failed to return the rental boat at&nbsp;
the appointed time, a team was sent to locate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:52.520
the boat and its passengers. When the boat&nbsp;
was found, only Rivera's son was onboard.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:54.600
The child told authorities that after a swim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:57.840
his mother helped him back onto the boat&nbsp;
before she slipped beneath the water's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:02.240
surface and disappeared. After an exhaustive&nbsp;
and multi-day search, the Ventura County&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:06.480
Sheriff's Office announced the discovery&nbsp;
of Rivera's body. According to E! Online,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:12.120
an autopsy concluded that Rivera died as a result&nbsp;
of accidental drowning. She was 33 years old.

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:15.840
Billed as "Ancient Librarian," Jean Sincere&nbsp;
appeared in three episodes of Glee during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:19.680
its first and second seasons. Her role&nbsp;
largely consisted of either shushing or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:23.668
glaring at the glee club members as they&nbsp;
broke into exuberant songs in her library.

00:04:23.668 --> 00:04:25.600
However, in Season 1's Bad Reputation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:29.080
the librarian responds to a performance&nbsp;
of "U Can't Touch This" by suggesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:32.480
that the club members recreate the song&nbsp;
for the Sunday service at her church.

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:36.640
The role of the Ancient Librarian was typical of&nbsp;
Sincere's television career, which began in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:42.000
late 1940s and concluded in 2012 with a guest&nbsp;
appearance on iCarly. A trained stage actress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:46.480
Sincere made her debut on Broadway in the&nbsp;
1941 production of Arsenic and Old Lace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:49.920
and appeared in other shows before&nbsp;
touring Europe as part of the USO.

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:53.040
After nearly three decades away from&nbsp;
television, Sincere returned to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:57.600
small screen in a 1985 episode of Cagney and&nbsp;
Lacey and moved into steady work as a guest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:01.000
player in series like Newhart, Murphy&nbsp;
Brown, and Everybody Loves Raymond.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:03.600
She also made occasional&nbsp;
forays into feature film work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:06.760
the most memorable of which was her&nbsp;
voice-acting role as the elderly Mrs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:10.720
Hogenson, whom Bob Parr helps with an&nbsp;
insurance claim in The Incredibles.

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:13.280
"I'm on a fixed income!"

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:17.240
Sincere remained active on television&nbsp;
and local theater into her ninth decade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:21.640
and died of natural causes at&nbsp;
the age of 93 on April 3, 2013.

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:25.040
Though some critics were not impressed by&nbsp;
"The Rocky Horror Glee Show," the Season 2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:29.000
tribute to Richard O'Brien's enduring&nbsp;
cult musical, fans of the 1975 film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:32.800
version were undoubtedly happy to see&nbsp;
two of its stars appear in minor roles.

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:36.360
Barry Bostwick, who played Brad in The&nbsp;
Rocky Horror Picture Show, and Meat Loaf,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:39.840
who played Eddie in both the film and the&nbsp;
original Broadway production, feature as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:44.520
managers of a local news station who plot with Sue&nbsp;
Sylvester to produce an expose on the school play.

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:48.280
Born Marvin Lee Aday, singer Meat Loaf&nbsp;
enjoyed chart success in three different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:52.120
decades as well as a second career as&nbsp;
an actor. His success on the pop and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:55.960
rock charts was largely anchored around&nbsp;
the Bat Out of Hell album trilogy. Some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:59.640
of the hits off these records include the&nbsp;
epically scaled "Paradise By the Dashboard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:03.160
Light," and the Grammy-winning "I'd Do&nbsp;
Anything for Love (But I Won't Do That").

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:07.360
As an actor, Meat Loaf's best-known role&nbsp;
outside of Rocky Horror was as Robert Paulson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:10.760
the sympathetic ex-weightlifter in&nbsp;
David Fincher's Fight Club. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:14.480
he also appeared in Wayne's World and The&nbsp;
Salton Sea, and made guest spots on shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:18.320
like Masters of Horror and Elementary.&nbsp;
After experiencing several health issues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:24.360
in the 2000s, he died at the age of 74 in&nbsp;
Nashville, Tennessee on January 20, 2022.

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:28.200
The character Pete Sosnowski presided&nbsp;
in part over the 2010 Western Ohio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:31.920
Sectionals Championship in Season 2's&nbsp;
"Special Education." This character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:35.720
played by veteran actor Mike Hagerty, is&nbsp;
probably best known as the man who announced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:39.760
that the New Directions and the Warblers tied&nbsp;
for first place at that year's competition.

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:44.320
A Chicago native and member of its famed improv&nbsp;
troupe, The Second City, Hagerty began appearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:48.840
in minor and supporting roles in both TV&nbsp;
and movies in the early '80s. A go-to for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:53.440
overworked and easily aggravated blue-collar&nbsp;
types, Hagerty played cops, truck drivers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:58.200
salesmen, and furniture movers on classic sitcoms&nbsp;
such as Married... with Children and Cheers.

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:02.160
By the mid-'90s, Hagerty had moved up&nbsp;
to supporting roles, which included his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:06.480
best-known small screen characters: grumpy&nbsp;
building supervisor Mr. Treeger on Friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:10.000
and the owner of a vintage clothing store&nbsp;
who butted heads with Kramer in Seinfeld.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:12.680
Hagerty remained active well into&nbsp;
the new millennium with recurring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:14.920
roles in Lucky Louie and Brooklyn Nine-Nine.

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:16.960
"What the hell's going on around here?"

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:19.518
"Fire extinguisher roller chair derby?"

00:07:19.518 --> 00:07:20.238
"Okay."

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:23.880
He also co-starred in the HBO&nbsp;
comedy Somebody Somewhere. TMZ&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:26.480
reported that after completing&nbsp;
the latter show's first season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:30.920
Hagerty experienced a seizure due to an adverse&nbsp;
reaction to antibiotics and lapsed into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:37.400
coma. The 67-year-old died at Los Angeles'&nbsp;
Cedars-Sinai Medical Center on May 5, 2022.

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:40.920
Singer and actress Olivia Newton-John&nbsp;
parodied her own squeaky-clean image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:45.080
in two appearances on Glee. In Season&nbsp;
1's "Bad Reputation," she helped repair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:48.400
the damage done to Sue Sylvester's&nbsp;
status and dignity after a bootleg&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:51.480
video of her performing Newton-John's&nbsp;
"Physical" was leaked to the internet.

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:54.880
To help Sue save face, Newton-John&nbsp;
appeared alongside her in an almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:57.320
exact duplicate of the song's original campy music&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.320 --> 00:08:00.680
video. She later returned that same&nbsp;
season for "Journey to Regionals",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:04.720
in which she played a self-centered judge for the&nbsp;
New Directions' performance at the competition.

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:08.600
Born in England but raised in Australia,&nbsp;
Newton-John enjoyed considerable success&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:14.440
in two mediums throughout the 1970s and 1980s.&nbsp;
As a singer, she earned 15 Top 10 singles on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.440 --> 00:08:20.000
Billboard's Hot 100 chart, including five No. 1&nbsp;
hits like "I Honestly Love You" and "Physical."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:24.800
In addition to topping the charts, she also&nbsp;
earned 12 Grammy nominations and four wins.

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:28.120
Newton-John's success as a singer also&nbsp;
led to an acting career that included&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:33.640
the staggering box office hit Grease — which&nbsp;
also generated three Top 10 hits — and Xanadu.

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:38.680
Diagnosed with breast cancer in 1992, Newton-John&nbsp;
became a tireless advocate for research and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.680 --> 00:08:44.320
treatment. The disease returned in 2017 and&nbsp;
metastasized to her back and bones.Newton-John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:50.200
died at the age of 73 at her home in the Santa&nbsp;
Ynez Valley in California on August 8, 2022.

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:53.720
Emmy-winning comic actor Tim Conway&nbsp;
joined fellow performing vets Billy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:58.040
Dee Williams and June Squibb for Season&nbsp;
5's "Old Dog, New Tricks." The episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:01.840
which was penned by cast member Chris Colfer,&nbsp;
gave its three guest stars a few moments to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:06.360
shine as residents at a home for retired actors&nbsp;
who are producing a stage version of Peter Pan.

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:10.240
Conway began in regional television in the&nbsp;
late 1950s before earning his first break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:14.520
on The Steve Allen Show. A co-starring role on&nbsp;
McHale's Navy led to several attempts to craft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:18.760
a TV series around him, but Conway always&nbsp;
seemed to fare best as a supporting player.

00:09:18.760 --> 00:09:23.120
He was a hilarious improviser who frequently&nbsp;
reduced his castmates on The Carol Burnett Show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:28.000
to hysterics, and teamed with another peerless TV&nbsp;
comic, Don Knotts, in a string of '70s features&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:32.200
for Walt Disney Pictures. Conway was also an&nbsp;
in-demand guest star on numerous series like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:37.640
Coach and 30 Rock, for which he won two additional&nbsp;
Emmys. He was also a popular voice-over actor for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:41.960
animated projects, most notably voicing&nbsp;
Barnacle Boy in SpongeBob SquarePants.

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:46.320
In 2018, Conway was diagnosed with dementia&nbsp;
due to normal pressure hydrocephalus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:50.080
a condition that causes cerebrospinal&nbsp;
fluid to build up in the brain. He died&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:55.600
from complications from the condition at the&nbsp;
age of 85 in Los Angeles on May 14, 2019.

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:59.920
Best known as the creator, writer, and host&nbsp;
of the long-running Bravo and Ovation series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:03.480
Inside the Actors Studio, James Lipton&nbsp;
appeared on Glee's Season 3 finale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:07.200
"Goodbye." The episode, which concerned&nbsp;
the graduation of eight Glee club members,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:11.280
saw Finn audition for Lipton in order to&nbsp;
win a spot at the prestigious Actors Studio.

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:16.080
That same year, Lipton also interviewed several&nbsp;
Glee cast members and co-creator Ryan Murphy on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:19.880
an episode of the Actors Studio series.&nbsp;
Inside the Actors Studio was the product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:23.720
of Lipton's work as the dean of the Actors&nbsp;
Studio Drama School, a graduate program for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.720 --> 00:10:27.440
actors that united the Actors Studio&nbsp;
with New York's New School University.

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:30.960
As part of its curriculum, Lipton recorded&nbsp;
interviews with actors, directors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:36.080
and writers about their crafts. These interviews&nbsp;
began airing on Bravo in 1994, and the show's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:41.040
success vaulted Lipton to pop culture stardom. The&nbsp;
host soon parlayed his success into appearances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:44.640
most often as himself, in series like&nbsp;
The Simpsons and Saturday Night Live.

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:46.720
Lipton was also an actor himself for a time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:50.560
beginning his career in radio during the&nbsp;
1940s and making appearances on Broadway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:54.920
and television during the 1950s. He segued into&nbsp;
writing for television series like The Guiding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.920 --> 00:10:59.160
Light and specials for comedian Bob Hope, as&nbsp;
well as several novels and Broadway musicals.

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:04.000
He remained with Inside the Actors Studio until&nbsp;
2018, earning 18 Emmy nominations during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:10.120
show's 24-year run. Lipton died of bladder&nbsp;
cancer at the age of 93 on March 2, 2020.

